p.src:
Only the matter inside the sphere of radius $a(t)$ contributes to the gravitational pull on the particle at $x=1$. Give that particle unit mass. Its kinetic energy is $\frac{1}{2}\dot a^2.$ The mass enclosed by the sphere is $M_{\mathrm{enc}}=\frac{4\pi}{3}a^3\rho,$ so the gravitational potential energy is $-\frac{G M_{\mathrm{enc}}}{a}
=
-\frac{4\pi G}{3}a^2\rho.$  (Editorial clarification: At lecture timestamp 00:05:30 the board clearly shows the kinetic term and the density-dependent potential term. The full coefficient written here follows from the spoken derivation.)

